- Can you adapt to compute even parity per byte instead of odd parity?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to extend known 64-bit bit tricks to 128-bit values without branches. Emphasizes portability and optimization.

## Key Concepts
- SWAR parallel bit counting
- Masks and shifts across 64-bit lanes
- Combining results from hi/lo halves

## Code Example
```javascript
static inline unsigned popcount64(uint64_t x) {
  x = x - ((x >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
  x = (x & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((x >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  x = (x + (x >> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  x = x + (x >> 8);
  x = x + (x >> 16);
  x = x + (x >> 32);
  return (unsigned)(x & 0x7F);
}
unsigned countBits128(uint128 x) {
  return popcount64(x.hi) + popcount64(x.lo);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you use hardware intrinsics if allowed?
- How would you extend to 256-bit types or streaming data?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests endianness handling and 128-bit manipulation with no branches.

## Key Concepts
- 64-bit byte reversal
- 128-bit composition
- Branchless bit-twiddling

## Code Example
```c
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;
static inline uint64_t reverseBytes64(uint64_t x) {
  x = ((x & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL) << 8) | ((x >> 8) & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL);
  x = ((x & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL) << 16) | ((x >> 16) & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL);
  x = (x << 32) | (x >> 32);
  return x;
}
uint128 mirrorBytes128(uint128 x) {
  uint128 y; y.hi = reverseBytes64(x.lo); y.lo = reverseBytes64(x.hi); return y;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt for streaming data where only a portion is available?
- What are the performance trade-offs of inlining reverseBytes64?

## Why This Is Asked
Endianness matters across 128-bit data paths; swapping bytes is a frequent low-level operation in serialization and network code.

## Key Concepts
- Endianness and cross-word byte reversal
- SWAR bit-twiddling for bswap64
- Handling 128-bit via hi/lo without 128-bit types

## Code Example
```c
static uint64_t swap64(uint64_t x) {
  x = ((x & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL) << 8) | ((x >> 8) & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL);
  x = ((x & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL) << 16) | ((x >> 16) & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL);
  x = (x << 32) | (x >> 32);
  return x;
}
void swapEndian128(uint64_t hi, uint64_t lo, uint64_t *out_hi, uint64_t *out_lo) {
  *out_hi = swap64(lo);
  *out_lo = swap64(hi);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you generalize to 256-bit values?
- How would you test correctness with random inputs?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to reason about cross-boundary extraction and 128-bit layouts; practical in serialization, bitboard ops, or crypto.

## Key Concepts

- Cross-lane bit extraction
- Masking and shifts
- Boundary checks

## Code Example

```c
uint32_t extractWord128(uint128 x, unsigned pos){
  switch(pos){
    case 0: return (uint32_t)(x.lo & 0xFFFFFFFFu);
    case 1: return (uint32_t)((x.lo >> 32) & 0xFFFFFFFFu);
    case 2: return (uint32_t)(x.hi & 0xFFFFFFFFu);
    default: return (uint32_t)((x.hi >> 32) & 0xFFFFFFFFu);
  }
}
```

