- Can you refactor to be branchless while preserving correctness?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests mastery of non-trivial bit hacks and understanding of 8×8 transpose, a common optimization in graphics, crypto, and compression. It checks for precise, branchless manipulation and correct use of masks.

## Key Concepts
- fixed-sequence bit swaps
- 8×8 bit matrix representation
- mask constants, shifts, no loops

## Code Example
```cpp
uint64_t transpose64(uint64_t x) {
    const uint64_t m1  = 0x5555555555555555ULL;
    x = (x & m1) << 1 | (x >> 1) & m1;
    const uint64_t m2  = 0x3333333333333333ULL;
    x = (x & m2) << 2 | (x >> 2) & m2;
    const uint64_t m4  = 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
    x = (x & m4) << 4 | (x >> 4) & m4;
    const uint64_t m8  = 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL;
    x = (x & m8) << 8 | (x >> 8) & m8;
    const uint64_t m16 = 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL;
    x = (x & m16) << 16 | (x >> 16) & m16;
    const uint64_t m32 = 0x00000000FFFFFFFFULL;
    x = (x & m32) << 32 | (x >> 32) & m32;
    return x;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test this with randomized 64-bit inputs to validate correctness?
- How would you generalize to 16×16 blocks with a 256-bit input stored in four 64-bit words?

## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates the ability to decompose a 128-bit operation into smaller parallel units and efficiently apply a well-known branchless counting technique.

## Key Concepts
- SWAR (SIMD Within A Register) popcount for 32-bit lanes
- 128-bit value decomposition into hi/lo components
- Branchless bit manipulation and final reduction

## Code Example
```c
unsigned int popcount32(unsigned int x) {
    x = x - ((x>>1)&0x55555555);
    x = (x&0x33333333)+((x>>2)&0x33333333);
    x = (x+(x>>4))&0x0F0F0F0F;
    return (x*0x01010101)>>24;
}

unsigned int popcount128(uint128 x) {
    return popcount32(x.lo) + popcount32(x.lo >> 32) + 
           popcount32(x.hi) + popcount32(x.hi >> 32);
}
```

## Quick Tests
```c
assert(popcount128(0) == 0);
assert(popcount128(UINT128_MAX) == 128);
assert(popcount128(0xF0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0) == 32);
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests the ability to implement a constant-time bit reversal to verify a binary palindrome using only bitwise ops.

## Key Concepts

- Palindrome property in binary
- Bit-reversal networks with masks
- Branch-free comparison

## Code Example

```javascript
// C-like implementation (illustrative)
int isBinaryPalindrome32(uint32_t n) {
  uint32_t x = n;
  x = ((x >> 1) & 0x55555555) | ((x & 0x55555555) << 1);
  x = ((x >> 2) & 0x33333333) | ((x & 0x33333333) << 2);
  x = ((x >> 4) & 0x0F0F0F0F) | ((x & 0x0F0F0F0F) << 4);
  x = ((x >> 8) & 0x00FF00FF) | ((x & 0x00FF00FF) << 8);
  x = (x >> 16) | (x << 16);
  return (x == n);
}
```

